  "answer_markdown": "Yes, Mistral Workflows documents a **default maximum of 100 concurrent token-stream tasks (activities) per workflow** when using the `max_concurrent_scheduled_tasks` parameter, which applies to both the **List Executor** and **Offset Pagination Executor** patterns [2]. This parameter controls how many activities can be scheduled simultaneously for execution, effectively acting as a concurrency limit for the workflow's token-stream tasks [2].\n\nIf this limit is exceeded, the workflow will **queue excess tasks** until available slots become free, preventing resource exhaustion but potentially delaying execution [2].\n\nFor the **Offset Pagination Executor**, an additional parameter, `max_concurrent_executions_per_worker`, further controls how many items are grouped into a single activity execution (default: 100), but this does not directly affect the overall concurrency limit [2].\n\n### Example Configuration\n```python\n# Limit to 50 concurrent tasks (instead of default 100)\nresults = await workflows.execute_activities_in_parallel(\n    activity=process_item,\n    items=items,\n    max_concurrent_scheduled_tasks=50  # Explicitly set lower concurrency\n)\n```\n\n### Key Notes\n- The **Chain Executor** does not use `max_concurrent_scheduled_tasks` and instead processes items sequentially [2].\n- Adjusting these parameters is critical for balancing performance and resource usage, especially for I/O-bound (e.g., API calls) or CPU-bound tasks [2].",
